WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.839
In atomic physics, the Electron Shell Model
describes the distribution of electrons on

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:10.800
the »energy shells« of an atom in the periodic
table of elements.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.320
For example, it can be used to understand
the electron distribution of an oxygen atom.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.300
We can see from the periodic table that an
oxygen atom has 8 electrons.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.850
But how are they distributed in the atom?

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.220
The shell model answers this question.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.369
Each shell represents a certain energy.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:35.320
The electrons can occupy these shells and
thus take on this energy.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.300
Each shell can only hold a certain number
of electrons.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.640
The electrons on the outermost shell of an
atom are known as valence electrons and they

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.770
play a crucial role in chemical reactions.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:55.110
The shells are labeled with letters: The innermost
(first) shell is called the K-shell.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.230
It can hold a maximum of 2 electrons.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.359
Then it is completely filled.

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:02.930
The second shell is called the L-shell.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.940
It can hold a maximum of 8 electrons.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.400
The third shell is called the M shell.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.100
It can hold a maximum of 18 electrons.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.340
The fourth shell is called the N-shell.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.540
It can hold a maximum of 32 electrons.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.320
How do I know the maximum number of electrons
a shell can hold?

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.320
There is a formula for this.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.720
Let's call the maximum number of electrons
that a shell can hold as "n_max".

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.220
You can then calculate this number using the
following formula: "n_max" is equal to two

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.650
n squared.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.170
Where "n" is the shell number.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.330
For the first shell, the K shell, "n equals
one".

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:47.330
For the second shell, the L-shell, "n equals
two" For the third shell, the M-shell, "n

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.290
equals three", &nbsp;and so on!

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.080
Let's take a look at four examples.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.270
First, the hydrogen atom.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:00.200
If you look at the periodic table, you can
read the number of electrons from the atomic

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.159
number in the corner.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.009
The hydrogen atom has an atomic number of
one.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:09.190
This means it only has one electron.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:16.050
We start with the innermost shell, the K shell,
and add an electron to it.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:21.720
As the hydrogen atom has no other electrons,
it only has a half-filled K-shell.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.440
The lithium atom has the atomic number three.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.900
This means it has three electrons.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.960
We start with the K shell.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.150
The K-shell can hold two electrons, so we
fill it with two electrons.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:42.170
The third electron is added to the next shell,
the L shell.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:46.680
The oxygen atom has eight electrons.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.780
Two are on the K shell.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:54.430
The L-shell can hold a maximum of eight electrons,
so the remaining six electrons are added to

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.260
it.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.360
The aluminum atom has thirteen electrons.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.659
Two electrons are added to the K-shell.

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:08.180
Eight electrons are added to the L-shell,
then the K-shell is full.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.630
The remaining three electrons are added to
the M-shell.

